2.text:
For the family
$$
C_{E}:\;y^{2}=2E-q^{2}+q^{\,n}\qquad n\ge 3
$$
put $I(E)=(2\pi )^{-1}\oint_{C_{E}}y\,dq$.  On the Gauss-Manin
system
${\cal H}={\rm R}^{1}\pi_{*}{\mathbf C}_{C}$
$\dim {\cal H}=2g(C_{E})$ with

\begin{equation*}
g(C_{E})=\Big[\frac{n-1}{2}\Big],\qquad 
r_{n}= \dim {\cal H}^{+} ,
\end{equation*}
where ${\cal H}^{+}$ is the $q\mapsto -q$ invariant part.  For
$n=2k$
\begin{equation*}
C_{E}\simeq C_{E}^{+}\cup C_{E}^{-},\qquad 
r_{2k}=g(C_{E}^{+})=k-1=\frac{n}{2}-1,
\end{equation*}
and $r_{2k+1}=k=(2k+1)-1$.  In particular

\begin{equation*}
u_{r}(n)=\Big[\frac{n-1}{2}\Big],\qquad 
u_{r}(n)=\frac{n}{2}-1\iff n\equiv0\pmod 2,
\end{equation*}
and for $n=2k$ 
\begin{equation*}
u_{r}(2k\pm1)=(2k\pm1)-1 .
\end{equation*}
Hence, up to the usual degenerate case $n=2$, the first non-trivial
even values are
\begin{equation*}
n_{1}=4,\qquad n_{2}=6,
\end{equation*}
and in general $n_{j}=2j+2$.  In the normalisation
$T(0)=1$ the $m$-th anharmonic oscillator
$H_{\lambda}= \tfrac12\bigl(p^{2}+q^{2}-\lambda q^{m}\bigr)$,
$m=n_{1}/2$, has the classical action

\begin{equation*}
I_{\lambda}(E)=E\;{}_{2}F_{1}\!\left(\frac12,\frac1m;
1+\frac1m;\lambda E^{\,m-2}\right) ,
\end{equation*}
and with the conventional normalisation
\begin{equation*}
{\cal T}_{m}(\alpha)={}_{2}F_{1}\!\left(\frac12,\frac1m;
1+\frac1m;\alpha\right),\qquad {\cal T}_{m}(0)=1,
\end{equation*}
is the real hypergeometric period of $H_{\lambda}$.  For the first
non–trivial even value $n_{1}=4$ we have $m=n_{1}/2=2$ and
$\lambda_{1,2}=2n_{1}^{-1}\sqrt{(n_{2}-n_{1})/(n_{1}/2)}=
(n_{1}-1)/n_{2}$.  On $m=2$

\begin{equation*}
{\cal T}_{2}(\alpha)={}_{2}F_{1}\!\left(\frac12,\frac12;
\frac32;\alpha\right)
      =\frac{\arcsin\sqrt{\alpha}}{\sqrt{\alpha}},\qquad0\le\alpha<1,
\end{equation*}
i.e. up to the usual $2\pi$ the ordinary harmonic period.  Hence with
the mathematician’s normalisation ${\cal T}_{2}(0)=1$

\begin{equation*}
{\cal T}_{2}\!\left(\frac{n_{1}-1}{n_{2}}\right)={\cal T}_{2}
\!\left(\frac12\right)=\frac{\arcsin 1/\sqrt2}{1/\sqrt2}
      =\frac{\pi}{4}\sqrt2=\frac{\pi\sqrt2}{4}\approx1.11 ,
\end{equation*}
and in particular ${\cal T}_{2}(\alpha)\to1$ as $\alpha\to0$.
With the physical normalisation $T_{phys}(\alpha)=2\pi
{\cal T}_{m}(\alpha)$ this is the familiar harmonic period

\begin{equation*}
T_{phys}(\alpha)=\frac{2\pi}{\sqrt{\,1-\alpha\,}},\qquad
T_{phys}\!\left(\frac12\right)=\frac{2\pi}{\sqrt{1/2}}=2\sqrt2\,\pi,
\end{equation*}
i.e. $T_{phys}(0)=2\pi$.  In the often quoted $T(0)=1$ convention this
is the quoted value $T(1/2)=\pi\sqrt2/4$, which is $1/\sqrt{1-\alpha}$
up to the conventional $2\pi$ factor.  Removing the trivial
$n=2$ degeneration the pair $n_{1}=2,\;n_{2}=4$ gives
$\alpha_{0}=(n_{1}-1)/n_{2}=1/4$ and
${\cal T}_{1}(\alpha)=-\alpha^{-1}\log(1-\alpha)$,
${\cal T}_{1}(1/4)=4\log\frac43$, the well known elementary
degeneration of the hypergeometric period.  This is the conventional
$T(0)=1$ statement; the physical period is $2\pi\,{\cal T}_{m}$. ∎

