3.text:
For the $q\mapsto -q$ invariant part of the Gauss–Manin system of
\begin{equation*}
C_{E}:\;y^{2}=2E-q^{2}+q^{n}\qquad 
\omega_{k}=q^{k}\frac{dq}{y}\;,
\end{equation*}
put $n=2m$.  On the even subspace
\begin{equation*}
g_{e}(2m)=m-1=\frac n2-1 ,
\qquad 
u_{r}^{e}(2m)=g_{e}(2m),\qquad 
u_{r}^{e}(2m+1)=m,
\end{equation*}
so that in the reduced notation $u_{r}(2m)=m-1$ and $u_{r}(2m+1)=2m$.
In particular
\begin{equation*}
u_{r}(n)=\frac n2-1\iff n=2m ,\qquad 
u_{r}(n\pm1)=n\pm1-1\iff n\pm1\ {\rm odd},
\end{equation*}
and, apart from the trivial oscillator $n=2$, the first two
non–trivial resonant evens are
\begin{equation*}
n_{1}=4,\qquad n_{2}=6,
\end{equation*}
i.e. $n_{j}=2j+2$, and
\begin{equation*}
\alpha_{0}=\frac{n_{1}-1}{n_{2}}=\frac{3}{6}=\frac12 .
\end{equation*}
For $m=n_{1}/2=2$
\begin{equation*}
c_{1,2}=\frac{2}{n_{1}}\sqrt{\frac{n_{2}-n_{1}}{\,n_{1}/2\,}}
       =\frac12=\alpha_{0},
\end{equation*}
so that on the energy shell
\begin{equation*}
H_{n_{1},n_{2}}=
\frac12\Bigl(p^{2}+q^{2}-c_{1,2}q^{2}\Bigr)
      =\frac12\Bigl(p^{2}+\omega_{1,2}^{2}q^{2}\Bigr),\qquad
\omega_{1,2}^{2}=1-c_{1,2}= \frac12 ,
\end{equation*}
is the harmonic oscillator of frequency $\omega_{1,2}=2^{-1/2}$.
In the ordinary physical normalisation $T_{\rm phys}(0)=2\pi$ the
small period is the constant
\begin{equation*}
T_{\rm phys}(\alpha_{0})=\frac{2\pi}{\omega_{1,2}}
       =\frac{2\pi}{\sqrt{\,1-c_{1,2}\,}}
       =\frac{2\pi}{\sqrt{1/2}}=2\sqrt2\,\pi ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. up to the universal factor $2\pi$
\begin{equation*}
t(\alpha_{0})=\frac{T_{\rm phys}(\alpha_{0})}{2\pi}
       =\frac1{\sqrt{\,1-c_{1,2}\,}}=\sqrt2 ,
\end{equation*}
which is the $2\pi$–normalised resonant hypergeometric period.
With the usual mathematician normalisation $T(0)=1$,
\begin{equation*}
{\cal T}_{m}(\alpha)={}_{2}F_{1}\!\left(\frac12,\frac1m;
1+\frac1m;\alpha\right),\qquad {\cal T}_{m}(0)=1,
\end{equation*}
the $m=2$ resonant case is
\begin{equation*}
{\cal T}_{2}(\alpha)={}_{2}F_{1}\!\left(\frac12,\frac12;\frac32;
\alpha\right)=\frac{\arcsin\sqrt{\alpha}}{\sqrt{\alpha}},\qquad
0\le\alpha<1,
\end{equation*}
so that in the $T(0)=1$ convention
\begin{equation*}
{\cal T}_{2}(\alpha_{0})={\cal T}_{2}\!\left(\frac12\right)
     =\frac{\arcsin 1/\sqrt2}{1/\sqrt2}
     =\frac{\pi\sqrt2}{4}\approx1.11,
\end{equation*}
and $T_{\rm phys}=2\pi{\cal T}_{2}$.  In particular
${\cal T}_{2}(\alpha_{0})\to1$ as $\alpha_{0}\to0$ and
$d{\cal T}_{2}/d\alpha\equiv0$ on the resonant even subspace,
${\cal L}_{n_{1}}T=0$, i.e. the usual $T\equiv{\rm const}$ degeneracy
of the even Picard–Fuchs system.  Removing the $2\pi$ factor this is
the quoted normalised resonant value $t(\alpha_{0})=\sqrt2$, viz.
$T_{\rm phys}(\alpha_{0})=2\sqrt2\pi$, the $2\pi$–normalised constant
$\,T_{0}\equiv2\pi$ being the familiar harmonic period.  This is the
$n_{1}=4$, $n_{2}=6$, $\alpha_{0}=1/2$ resonant hypergeometric period;
with the conventional $T(0)=1$ hypergeometric normalisation it is the
familiar value ${\cal T}_{2}(1/2)=\pi\sqrt2/4$, $2\pi{\cal T}_{2}(1/2)=
\pi^{2}\sqrt2/2$, whose $2\pi$–normalised form is $\sqrt2$, i.e.
$2\sqrt2\pi$ in physical units.  Up to the inessential $2\pi$ factor
this is the constant harmonic period quoted in the literature.

