5.text:
In the $q\mapsto -q$ invariant part put $y^{2}=2E-q^{2}+q^{n}$ and
$\omega _{k}=q^{k}dq/y$.  With the $2g$ convention

\begin{equation*}
u_{r}(n)=2\Big[\frac{n-1}{2}\Big]=\left\{\,n-2\;n\;{\rm even},
\;n-1\;n\;{\rm odd}\right. ,
\qquad u^{e}_{r}(n)=\Big[\frac{n-1}{2}\Big] .
\end{equation*}
Hence on the even sub-system $n=2m$

\begin{equation*}
u^{e}_{r}(2m)=m-1=\frac n2-1,\qquad 
u^{e}_{r}(2m\pm 1)=m ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. $u_{r}(n)=n/2-1$ is the even resonant drop and
$u_{r}(n\pm1)=n\pm1-1$ the ordinary odd order on the two neighbours.
Apart from the trivial oscillator the first non trivial even
resonances are

\begin{equation*}
n_{1}=2\cdot2=4,\qquad n_{2}=2\cdot3=6,\qquad n_{j}=2j+2,
\end{equation*}
and in the usual $2g$ trivial labelling $n_{1}^{0}=2,\;n_{2}^{0}=4$.
In the non–trivial labelling

\begin{equation*}
\alpha_{0}:=\frac{n_{1}-1}{n_{2}}=\frac{3}{6}=\frac12,\qquad 
m=\frac{n_{1}}2=2,
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
c_{1,2}=\frac{2}{n_{1}}\sqrt{\frac{n_{2}-n_{1}}{n_{1}/2}}
      =\frac12=\alpha_{0}.
\end{equation*}
On the energy shell

\begin{equation*}
H_{n_{1},n_{2}}=\frac12\Bigl(p^{2}+q^{2}-c_{1,2}q^{m}\Bigr)
   =\frac12\Bigl(p^{2}+(1-c_{1,2})q^{2}\Bigr)
   =\frac12\Bigl(p^{2}+\omega_{0}^{2}q^{2}\Bigr),\qquad
\omega_{0}^{2}=1-c_{1,2}=\frac12 ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. the even resonant Hamiltonian is the harmonic oscillator with
$\omega_{0}=2^{-1/2}$.  On the even resonant line $m-2=0$ the
Gauss–Manin system collapses to

\begin{equation*}
{\cal L}_{n_{1}}T=\frac{dT}{d\alpha}=0,
\end{equation*}
so that with the mathematician normalisation ${\cal T}_{m}(0)=1$

\begin{equation*}
{\cal T}_{m}(\alpha)={}_{2}F_{1}\!\left(\frac12,\frac1m;
1+\frac1m;\alpha\right),\qquad {\cal T}_{m}(0)=1,
\qquad 
I_{\lambda}(E)=E\,{\cal T}_{m}\!\bigl(\lambda E^{\,m-2}\bigr),
\end{equation*}
the reduced period is $\alpha$–independent on the resonant even
sub-sequence.  For $m=2$

\begin{equation*}
{\cal T}_{2}(\alpha)={}_{2}F_{1}\!\left(\frac12,\frac12;
\frac32;\alpha\right)=\frac{\arcsin\sqrt{\alpha}}{\sqrt{\alpha}},\qquad
0\le\alpha<1,\;\;{\cal T}_{2}(0)=1,
\end{equation*}
so that in the $T(0)=1$ convention the reduced solution is the
constant

\begin{equation*}
{\cal T}_{n_{1}}(\alpha)\equiv{\cal T}_{n_{1}}(0)=1,
\qquad {\cal L}_{n_{1}}{\cal T}_{n_{1}}=0,
\end{equation*}
on the even resonant line.  In particular

\begin{equation*}
{\cal T}_{n_{1}}(\alpha_{0})={\cal T}_{n_{1}}(0)=1,
\end{equation*}
the familiar $dT/d\alpha=0$ degeneracy, i.e. up to the universal
$2\pi$ factor the $2\pi$–free reduced hypergeometric period is
constant on the even resonant sub-family $n_{j}=2j+2$.  In the
unreduced $2F_{1}$ normalisation this is the explicit value

\begin{equation*}
{\cal T}_{2}(\alpha_{0})={\cal T}_{2}\!\left(\tfrac12\right)
   =\frac{\arcsin 1/\sqrt2}{1/\sqrt2}
   =\frac{\pi\sqrt2}{4}\approx1.11,
\end{equation*}
and with the physical $T_{\rm phys}(0)=2\pi$ normalisation

\begin{equation*}
T_{\rm phys}(\alpha_{0})=\frac{2\pi}{\omega_{0}}
   =\frac{2\pi}{\sqrt{\,1-c_{1,2}\,}}
   =\frac{2\pi}{\sqrt{1-\alpha_{0}}}=2\sqrt2\,\pi,
\qquad 
\frac{T_{\rm phys}(\alpha_{0})}{2\pi}=\frac1{\sqrt{1-\alpha_{0}}}
      =\sqrt2,
\end{equation*}
the ordinary harmonic period of 
$H_{4,6}=\tfrac12\!\left(p^{2}+\tfrac12q^{2}\right)$.  Discarding the
inessential overall $2\pi$ this is the statement ${\cal T}_{n_{1}}\equiv1$,
$dT/d\alpha=0$ on the even resonant line.  With the trivial $n=2$
shift this is $n_{1}^{0}=2,\;n_{2}^{0}=4,\;\alpha_{0}^{0}=1/4$ and
${\cal T}\equiv1$, $T_{\rm phys}^{0}=2\pi$.  This is the $n_{1}=4,\,
n_{2}=6,\;\alpha_{0}=1/2$ even resonant hypergeometric degeneracy.
∎

1.text:
In the $q\mapsto -q$ invariant part
\begin{equation*}
C_{E}:y^{2}=2E-q^{2}+q^{n},\qquad \omega_{k}=q^{k}dq/y ,
\end{equation*}
put $g_{e}(n)=\big[\,(n-1)/2\,\big]$.  In the reduced $g_{e}$
normalisation $u_{r}^{e}(n)=g_{e}(n)$ and in the $2g$ convention
$u_{r}(n)=2g_{e}(n)$

\begin{equation*}
u_{r}(n)=\left\{\,n-1\;n\;{\rm odd}\atop n-2\;n\;{\rm even}\right.,\qquad
u_{r}^{e}(2m)=m-1=\frac n2-1,\qquad u_{r}^{e}(2m\pm1)=m,
\end{equation*}
so that $u_{r}(n)=n/2-1$ is the even resonant drop and
$u_{r}(n\pm1)=n\pm1-1$ is the ordinary odd order on the two
neighbours.  Apart from the trivial oscillator $n=2$ the first two
non–trivial even resonances are

\begin{equation*}
n_{1}=2\cdot2=4,\qquad n_{2}=2\cdot3=6,\qquad n_{j}=2j+2,
\end{equation*}
the trivial $2g$ labelling being $n_{1}^{0}=2,\;n_{2}^{0}=4$.
Put $m=n_{1}/2=2$ and

\begin{equation*}
\alpha_{0}:=\frac{n_{1}-1}{n_{2}}=\frac{3}{6}=\frac12,\qquad
\lambda_{0}=c_{n_{1},n_{2}}=
\frac{2}{n_{1}}\sqrt{\frac{n_{2}-n_{1}}{n_{1}/2}}=\frac12
=\alpha_{0},
\end{equation*}
so that on the energy shell

\begin{equation*}
H_{n_{1},n_{2}}=\frac12\Bigl(p^{2}+q^{2}-\lambda_{0}q^{m}\Bigr)
 =\frac12\Bigl(p^{2}+(1-\lambda_{0})q^{2}\Bigr)
 =\frac12\Bigl(p^{2}+\omega_{0}^{2}q^{2}\Bigr),\qquad 
\omega_{0}^{2}=1-\lambda_{0}=\frac12,
\end{equation*}
i.e. the even resonant Hamiltonian is the harmonic oscillator
$\omega_{0}=2^{-1/2}$.  On the even sub–system $m-2=0$ the
Picard–Fuchs operator collapses to

\begin{equation*}
{\cal L}_{n_{1}}T=\frac{dT}{d\alpha}=0,
\end{equation*}
so that with the mathematician normalisation ${\cal T}_{m}(0)=1$

\begin{equation*}
I_{\lambda}(E)=E\,{\cal T}_{m}\!\bigl(\lambda E^{\,m-2}\bigr),\qquad
{\cal T}_{m}(\alpha)={}_{2}F_{1}\!\left(\tfrac12,\tfrac1m;
1+\tfrac1m;\alpha\right),\quad {\cal T}_{m}(0)=1,
\end{equation*}
is the reduced period.  For $m=2$

\begin{equation*}
{\cal T}_{2}(\alpha)={}_{2}F_{1}\!\left(\tfrac12,\tfrac12;
\tfrac32;\alpha\right)=\frac{\arcsin\sqrt{\alpha}}{\sqrt{\alpha}},\qquad
0\le\alpha<1,
\end{equation*}
and ${\cal L}_{2}{\cal T}_{2}\equiv0$ on the even resonant line,
${\cal T}_{n_{1}}(\alpha)\equiv{\cal T}_{n_{1}}(0)$.
In particular in the $T(0)=1$ hypergeometric normalisation

\begin{equation*}
{\cal T}_{2}(\alpha_{0})={\cal T}_{2}\!\left(\tfrac12\right)
 =\frac{\arcsin 1/\sqrt2}{1/\sqrt2}
 =\frac{\pi\sqrt2}{4}\approx1.11,
\end{equation*}
the usual $2F_{1}$ value.  Removing the universal $2\pi$ the
$2\pi$-normalised reduced period is

\begin{equation*}
t(\alpha_{0})=\frac{T_{\rm phys}(\alpha_{0})}{2\pi}
       =\frac1{\sqrt{1-\lambda_{0}}}
       =\frac1{\sqrt{1-\alpha_{0}}}=\sqrt2,
\end{equation*}
i.e. in the physical $T_{\rm phys}(0)=2\pi$ normalisation

\begin{equation*}
T_{\rm phys}(\alpha_{0})=\frac{2\pi}{\omega_{0}}
