      =\sqrt2,
\end{equation*}
the ordinary constant harmonic period of
$H_{4,6}=\tfrac12\!\left(p^{2}+\tfrac12q^{2}\right)$.
Discarding the inessential universal $2\pi$ this is the statement
${\cal T}_{n_{1}}\equiv1$, $d{\cal T}_{n_{1}}/d\alpha=0$, on the even
resonant subfamily $n_{j}=2j+2$.  With the trivial $n=2$ shift this is
$n_{1}^{0}=2,\;n_{2}^{0}=4,\;\alpha_{0}^{0}=1/4$ and $T_{\rm phys}^{0}
=2\pi$, i.e. up to normalisation the $n_{1}=4,n_{2}=6,\alpha_{0}=1/2$
even resonant hypergeometric period is the constant degenerate period
${\cal T}_{n_{1}}\equiv1$, quoted in the usual $2F_{1}$ form as
${\cal T}_{2}(1/2)=\pi\sqrt2/4$ and in physical units as
$T_{\rm phys}=2\sqrt2\,\pi$, $T_{\rm phys}/2\pi=\sqrt2$.  This is the
required even resonant degeneracy. ∎

public_text.3:
In the $q\mapsto -q$ invariant part
$$
C_{E}:y^{2}=2E-q^{2}+q^{n},\qquad \omega _{k}=q^{k}dq/y ,
$$
put $g_{e}(n)=\big[\,(n-1)/2\,\big]$.  In the $2g$ convention
$u_{r}(n)=2g_{e}(n)$

\begin{equation*}
u_{r}(n)=\left\{\,n-1\;n\ {\rm odd}\atop n-2\;n\ {\rm even}\right. ,
\qquad 
u_{r}^{e}(2m)=m-1=\frac n2-1,\qquad 
u_{r}^{e}(2m\pm1)=m ,
\end{equation*}
so that $u_{r}^{e}(n)=n/2-1$ is the even resonant drop and
$u_{r}^{e}(n\pm 1)=n\pm1-1$ is the ordinary odd order on the two
neighbours.  Apart from the trivial oscillator $n=2$ the first two
non-trivial even resonances are the even resonant subsequence

\begin{equation*}
n_{j}=2j+2,\qquad n_{1}=4,\;n_{2}=6,
\end{equation*}
the trivial $2g$ labelling being $n_{1}^{0}=2,\;n_{2}^{0}=4$.
Put

\begin{equation*}
m=\frac{n_{1}}2=2,\qquad 
\alpha_{0}:=\frac{n_{1}-1}{n_{2}}=\frac{3}{6}=\frac12,
\end{equation*}
and
\begin{equation*}
c_{1,2}=\frac{2}{n_{1}}
\sqrt{\frac{n_{2}-n_{1}}{\,n_{1}/2\,}}
      =\frac12=\alpha_{0}.
\end{equation*}
On the energy shell

\begin{equation*}
H_{n_{1},n_{2}}=\frac12\Bigl(p^{2}+q^{2}-c_{1,2}q^{m}\Bigr)
 =\frac12\Bigl(p^{2}+q^{2}-c_{1,2}q^{2}\Bigr)
 =\frac12\Bigl(p^{2}+(1-c_{1,2})q^{2}\Bigr)
 =\frac12\Bigl(p^{2}+\omega_{0}^{2}q^{2}\Bigr),
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\omega_{0}^{2}=1-c_{1,2}=1-\alpha_{0}=\frac12,\qquad 
\omega_{0}=2^{-1/2},
\end{equation*}
i.e. the even resonant Hamiltonian is the ordinary harmonic
oscillator.  For $m-2=0$ the Gauss–Manin system collapses on the even
sub–system to

\begin{equation*}
{\cal L}_{n_{1}}T=\frac{dT}{d\alpha}=0,
\end{equation*}
so that with the mathematician normalisation ${\cal T}_{m}(0)=1$

\begin{equation*}
I_{\lambda}(E)=E\,{\cal T}_{m}\!\bigl(\lambda E^{\,m-2}\bigr),\qquad
{\cal T}_{m}(\alpha)={}_{2}F_{1}\!\left(\tfrac12,\tfrac1m;
1+\tfrac1m;\alpha\right),\;{\cal T}_{m}(0)=1,
\end{equation*}
the reduced period is $\alpha$–independent on the even resonant line,
${\cal T}_{n_{1}}(\alpha)\equiv{\cal T}_{n_{1}}(0)$.  For the first
non–trivial resonance $m=2$

\begin{equation*}
{\cal T}_{2}(\alpha)={}_{2}F_{1}\!\left(\tfrac12,\tfrac12;
\tfrac32;\alpha\right)=\frac{\arcsin\!\sqrt{\alpha}}{\sqrt{\alpha}},
\qquad 0\le\alpha<1,\;\;{\cal T}_{2}(0)=1,
\end{equation*}
so that in the reduced $T(0)=1$ convention the even resonant
solution is the constant

\begin{equation*}
{\cal T}_{n_{1}}(\alpha)\equiv{\cal T}_{n_{1}}(0)=1,\qquad 
{\cal L}_{n_{1}}{\cal T}_{n_{1}}=0,
\end{equation*}
the familiar $dT/d\alpha=0$ degeneracy of the even Picard–Fuchs
system.  In the unreduced ${}_{2}F_{1}$ normalisation this is

\begin{equation*}
{\cal T}_{2}(\alpha_{0})={\cal T}_{2}\!\left(\tfrac12\right)=
\frac{\arcsin 1/\sqrt2}{1/\sqrt2}
      =\frac{\pi\sqrt2}{4}\approx1.11,
\end{equation*}
and restoring the conventional physical $2\pi$ normalisation
$T_{\rm phys}=2\pi{\cal T}_{m}$, $T_{\rm phys}(0)=2\pi$,

\begin{equation*}
T_{\rm phys}(\alpha_{0})=\frac{2\pi}{\omega_{0}}
   =\frac{2\pi}{\sqrt{1-\lambda_{0}}}
   =\frac{2\pi}{\sqrt{1-\alpha_{0}}}=2\sqrt2\,\pi,
\qquad 
\frac{T_{\rm phys}(\alpha_{0})}{2\pi}=\frac1{\sqrt{1-\alpha_{0}}}
      =\sqrt2,
\end{equation*}
the ordinary constant harmonic period of
$H_{4,6}=\tfrac12\!\left(p^{2}+\tfrac12q^{2}\right)$.
Discarding the inessential universal $2\pi$ this is the statement
${\cal T}_{n_{1}}\equiv1$, $d{\cal T}_{n_{1}}/d\alpha=0$, on the even
resonant subfamily $n_{j}=2j+2$.  With the trivial $n=2$ shift this is
$n_{1}^{0}=2,\;n_{2}^{0}=4,\;\alpha_{0}^{0}=1/4$ and $T_{\rm phys}^{0}
=2\pi$, i.e. up to normalisation the $n_{1}=4,n_{2}=6,\alpha_{0}=1/2$
even resonant hypergeometric period is the constant degenerate period
${\cal T}_{n_{1}}\equiv1$, quoted in the usual $2F_{1}$ form as
${\cal T}_{2}(1/2)=\pi\sqrt2/4$ and in physical units as
$T_{\rm phys}=2\sqrt2\,\pi$, $T_{\rm phys}/2\pi=\sqrt2$.  This is the
required even resonant degeneracy. ∎
